WEBVTT

NOTE Space Shuttle Columbia: The Second Flight (1982) - Riah TV captions

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
[music]

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:20.100
April 1981.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.030
Columbia, the first space
shuttle orbiter glides to a

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.080
pinpoint landing at Edwards Air
Force Base, California, ending

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.270
its spectacular maiden flight.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:34.710
But unlike spacecraft
that had gone before,

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.350
Columbia would not be retired
from service to become a museum

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:39.480
piece.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:43.950
Its future as a space transport
vehicle had only begun.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.590
After its successful first
flight, Columbia was carried

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.460
back to Kennedy Space Center.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.410
There, the process of
turnaround was set in motion.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.320
There were system checkouts,

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.520
minor tile repairs
and replacements.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.950
An experiment's package called
OSTA -1 was placed in the

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.300
payload bay.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.050
The package rests on a platform
built by the European Space

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.400
Agency.

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.740
The Canadian -built remote
manipulator system was

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:22.090
installed.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:26.130
The system includes a 50
-foot long mechanical arm.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:32.260
In early August, Columbia
was moved over to the vehicle

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:33.610
assembly building.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.310
Here it was mated to two new
solid rocket boosters and an

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:41.920
external fuel tank.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:48.380
Three weeks later, it was rolled
to the launch pad once again.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.890
Shuttle flight one
brought many firsts.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:55.770
Shuttle flight two
would bring another.

00:01:57.010 --> 00:02:00.770
The first time in the history
of spaceflight that an attempt

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:05.560
would be made to launch the
same spacecraft a second time.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:06.910
During

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:26.800
the next three weeks, launch
preparation crews worked in

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:30.270
three shifts trying to meet
the early October launch date.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.150
All had gone smoothly up to the
point of loading the hypergolic

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.500
fuels.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.390
That process had
barely begun when

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:46.120
nitrogen tetroxide was
discovered spilling onto the

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.470
orbiter.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:52.180
Approximately 15 to 20 gallons
of the oxidizer had spilled down

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:55.640
the side of the vehicle and into
the propellant tank compartment

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:56.900
of the nose section.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.250
It

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:01.450
had also seeped between the
orbiter's thermal protection

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.470
system tiles and dissolved the
bond holding the tiles on the

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.820
spacecraft.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.020
Eventually, over 350
had to be removed.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.130
Cause of the spill was failure
of a quick disconnect valve to

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:18.480
close.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.900
The valve could not close
because of concentrations of

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:25.710
iron nitrate in the head and
hardening of lubrication in that

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:27.060
area.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:32.230
With Columbia still on the
launch pad, Workers were able to

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.180
gain access inside the nose
compartment and replace soaked

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:37.530
thermal blankets.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.880
They were also able to
reach the soaked tiles.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.870
Therefore, it would not be
necessary to move back to the

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.640
orbiter processing
facility for repairs.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.030
The tiles were put through a
very speedy decontamination

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:55.620
process to remove the oxidizer.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:04.740
Within three weeks after the
spill, all 378 tiles had been

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:08.700
removed, decontaminated,
and rebonded.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:12.920
Launch was rescheduled
for November 4th,

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:18.330
but Columbia was not destined
to return to space yet.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:23.010
Only 31 seconds away from
ignition, the launch computer

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:24.620
halted the countdown.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.330
The pressure in the liquid
oxygen tank was below the

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:31.000
predetermined limit
necessary for liftoff.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.980
Meanwhile, controllers were also
becoming increasingly concerned

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:39.940
about the high oil pressure in
two of the three auxiliary power

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.250
units.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:45.250
After analyzing this problem
further, they decided to scrub

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:46.780
the launch for the day.

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:53.510
The APUs are vital to Columbia's
safe ascent and entry for

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:57.140
steering the engines during
liftoff, controlling the arrow

00:04:57.190 --> 00:05:01.040
surfaces during landing, and
for lowering the landing gear.

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:04.560
It was suspected that oil
pressure rose in the units

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.710
because the filters
were clogged.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:11.360
The problem would have to be
solved before another launch.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:16.290
Analysis revealed that a wax
-like substance had formed in

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:17.730
the oil to clog the filters.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:22.160
The substance was created by a
chemical reaction between the

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:24.350
oil and hydrazine fuel.

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:28.860
The hydrazine seeped into the
gearbox over the seven month

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:34.190
period since the APUs were last
used and contaminated the oil.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.380
Although many thought
technicians simply forgot to

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:42.940
change the oil, normal
maintenance procedures did not

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.290
require it.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:48.690
Indeed, a key factor in
shuttle's reusability is quick

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:50.670
turnaround with
minimum maintenance.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:52.020
The

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:54.930
APUs were flushed
with new lube oil.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:58.320
New filters were installed
and fresh oil was added.

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:02.520
Working on a very tight
schedule, ground crews completed

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:03.950
the work within a week.

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.990
The shuttle was again
ready to be launched.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:15.090
Astronauts Joe Engel and Richard
Trulley, commander and pilot for

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:16.620
the second shuttle flight.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:27.940
Commander Engel, who joined NASA
in 1966, was part of the backup

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:31.810
crew for Apollo 14 and
the first shuttle flight.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:38.170
Three of his 16 X -15 flights
went above 50 miles, qualifying

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:42.840
him for astronaut rank, but
he had not yet been in orbit,

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:47.630
nor had Richard truly
pilot for this flight.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:53.120
Truly, who joined NASA in 1969,
was capsule communicator for

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:57.190
three Skylab missions and
the Apollo -Soyuz mission.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:01.090
He was also part of the backup
crew for Columbia's first

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:02.440
flight.

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:06.780
Both men had flown the shuttle
before in approach and landing

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:10.370
tests at Dryden Flight
Research Center in 1977.

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:13.810
That was over four years ago.

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:17.060
It was now 1981,

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:23.750
November 12th to be exact,
Richard Trulie's 44th birthday.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:29.730
Countdown now being conducted
by Z1 sequencers on board the

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:30.710
orbiters.

