Columbia Houston, you're going
for nominal ohms 1, for APU shut

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:14.830
down on time.

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:20.560
As

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:27.250
Columbia continued toward orbit,
the SRBs parachuted to a safe

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:31.630
landing in the Atlantic Ocean,
160 miles downrange from the

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:32.980
launch site.

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:36.640
They were recovered and will be
used again on a later shuttle

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:37.990
flight.

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:42.430
All orbital maneuvering system
burns were successful, putting

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:46.700
Columbia into a 138 -mile
orbit around the Earth.

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:54.180
The payload bay doors were open
to deploy the heat dissipating

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:58.240
radiators and expose
the OSTA experiments.

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:03.570
So began the many tasks of
the five -day mission that lay

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:04.920
ahead.

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:11.390
However, not long after
achieving orbit, at about 2

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:15.830
hours, 27 minutes, mission
elapsed time, the crew reported

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:20.830
a high pH level, indicating
alkalinity in one of Columbia's

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:22.380
three fuel cells.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:26.010
The cells, which produce
electricity and water for the

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:29.510
spacecraft and crew, are
essential for operating the many

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:30.960
systems on board.

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:35.360
Flight controllers, having dealt
with almost every conceivable

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:38.890
problem during flight
simulations, immediately began

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:42.020
working to find a real -time
solution to this problem.

00:12:43.190 --> 00:12:46.830
But meanwhile, the cell's
capacity to generate electricity

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:49.140
continued to deteriorate.

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:54.150
At about five hours' mission
elapsed time, it was decided to

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:59.250
take the fuel cell offline and
safe it, train it of all its

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:00.600
energy.

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:03.850
This would prevent a potentially
dangerous reaction from

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:06.320
occurring that could
damage the spacecraft.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:11.470
But according to a mission rule
made preflight, the failure of a

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:17.290
fuel cell would reduce the 125
-hour nominal mission to a 54

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:19.160
-hour minimum mission.

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:24.880
A mission management meeting
was held to decide whether to

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:30.260
enforce or override the rule,
Whether to come home after 54

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:34.100
hours or stay up for
the full duration at

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:39.720
a press conference on day two of
the mission Johnson Space Center

00:13:39.770 --> 00:13:43.140
director Christopher Kraft
Explained why the decision was

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:45.990
finally made to
shorten the mission.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:49.050
Well, we certainly don't have
any other problems on board I

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:52.250
think that we think it's the
prudent thing to do at this

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:53.840
point in our test program

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:57.400
We think we can really get
everything out of the mission

00:13:57.450 --> 00:14:02.150
that we have planned with the
with the exception of time So,

00:14:02.890 --> 00:14:06.450
we just felt from a
more prudent position,

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:10.890
we had thought it out very
carefully pre -flight that that

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:12.540
was the best thing for us to do.

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:16.670
Indeed, virtually everything was
gotten out of the mission that

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:18.020
was planned.

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:22.520
All high -priority objectives
were accomplished, and 90 % of

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:25.740
the overall objectives were
completed, even though this was

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:28.980
a test vehicle on a
developmental flight cut short

00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:31.250
71 hours of

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:37.170
top priority, with a remote
manipulator system tests,

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:39.490
completed on the
morning of day two.

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:45.850
Sally Ride, first female
capsule communicator, guided the

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:47.760
astronauts through the tests.

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:54.690
We've got a great picture from
camera delta of the arm going

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:56.040
down into the cradle.

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:57.700
Good,

00:15:00.330 --> 00:15:03.150
and out the window is the
best alignment device I think.

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:08.100
Now we've got a picture
of the PLT moving the arm.

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:16.840
The RMS, which is a major
subsystem of the orbiter, is

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:21.060
designed to deploy and retrieve
satellites and other payloads in

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:22.490
operational flights.

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:28.940
Although the arm did not
actually grapple a payload, the

00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:32.140
end effector was able to come
within six inches of a target

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:37.180
and maintain that position, two
very important objectives of the

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:38.530
test.

00:15:43.940 --> 00:15:44.750
Okay, we'll do that.

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:47.190
We've got a picture of the flag
back and we'll coordinate with

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:48.540
INCO.

